  <div class="guide-text-block">
    <h2>What Is Number 57 Stone?</h2>
    <p>
      In civil construction and aggregate quarrying, <strong>Number 57 Stone</strong> (often designated simply as #57 stone or #57 gravel) is one of the most widely specified coarse aggregate sizes in North America. Contrary to popular belief, the number 57 does not refer to a 57 millimeter particle diameter. Instead, it designates a standardized grading envelope established under <strong>ASTM D448</strong> (<em>Standard Classification for Sizes of Aggregate for Road and Bridge Construction</em>) and its highway equivalent, <strong>AASHTO M43</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The size number describes gradation, not mineral type or particle shape. Suppliers sell both crushed angular and naturally rounded products as #57 stone. Ask for the sieve gradation, source material, and fines content for the product you intend to use.
    </p>

    <h2>ASTM D448 and AASHTO M43 Gradation Sieve Envelope</h2>
    <p>
      A product sold to the ASTM D448 #57 size designation can be checked by laboratory sieve analysis under <strong>ASTM C136</strong> (<em>Standard Test Method for Sieve Analysis of Fine and Coarse Aggregates</em>). Its aggregate sample must fall within the applicable percentage-passing limits by dry weight:
    </p>

    <div class="table-container">
      <table class="guide-data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Standard Sieve Size</th>
            <th scope="col">Metric Opening (mm)</th>
            <th scope="col">Specification: Percent Passing by Weight</th>
            <th scope="col">Quarry Function in Aggregate Matrix</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>1.5 Inch (37.5 mm)</strong></td>
            <td>37.5 mm</td>
            <td><strong>100%</strong></td>
            <td>Maximum upper boundary; zero oversize particles allowed</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>1.0 Inch (25.0 mm)</strong></td>
            <td>25.0 mm</td>
            <td><strong>95% to 100%</strong></td>
            <td>Primary top caliper stone; carries compressive load</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>1/2 Inch (12.5 mm)</strong></td>
            <td>12.5 mm</td>
            <td><strong>25% to 60%</strong></td>
            <td>Intermediate aggregate fraction; bridges open void spaces</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>No. 4 Sieve (4.75 mm)</strong></td>
            <td>4.75 mm</td>
            <td><strong>0% to 10%</strong></td>
            <td>Fine stone chips; limited to preserve void conductivity</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>No. 8 Sieve (2.36 mm)</strong></td>
            <td>2.36 mm</td>
            <td><strong>0% to 5%</strong></td>
            <td>Mineral dust limit; washed to eliminate silt and clay</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Bulk Density and Quarry Weight Conversion</h2>
    <p>
<<<<<<< HEAD
      Suppliers may sell #57 products by loose cubic yard, US short ton or bag. For a weight-based quote, one US short ton equals 2,000 pounds; convert volume using the actual product’s loose bulk weight.
=======
      Some suppliers sell #57 stone by cubic yard and others by US short ton (2,000 pounds). Convert between the two using the loose bulk weight quoted for the actual product.
>>>>>>> 57e3bdaba9288075f4f22a663c6c2a65fa9f9ee3
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>ASTM C29 / C29M</strong> is a method for measuring bulk unit weight, not a universal #57 density. For comparison, <a href="https://southbaymaterials.com/drain-rock-3-4">South Bay lists 2,500 lb/cu yd</a> for one 3/4-inch washed drain rock, while <a href="https://shop.three-z.com/index.php?product_id=58&amp;route=product%2Fproduct">Three Z lists about 2,800 lb/cu yd</a> for a washed #57 gravel. The latter is rounded, so #57 does not always mean angular stone.
    </p>

    <div class="formula-callout">
      <code>Cubic Yards = (Square Feet × Depth in Inches) / 324</code>
      <br />
      <code>Estimated US Short Tons = Cubic Yards × Supplier lb/cu yd ÷ 2,000</code>
      <br />
      <code>At 2,700 lb/cu yd: 1 Ton Covers 120 sq ft at 2" Depth</code>
      <br />
      <code>At 2,700 lb/cu yd: 1 Ton Covers 80 sq ft at 3" Depth</code>
    </div>

    <p>
      <strong>Stockpile condition matters:</strong> Moisture and gradation can change the delivered weight of a cubic yard. Confirm the yard's estimate when ordering and use the <a href="/rock-density-chart">density chart</a> as a comparison, not a substitute for the quote.
    </p>

    <h2>Primary Construction and Landscaping Applications</h2>
    
    <h3>1. French Drains and Subsurface Drainage Systems</h3>
    <p>
      A clean, appropriately graded #57 product can be used around a perforated pipe where the project detail allows it. Check that the stone is supplied with suitably low fines; the size number alone does not guarantee it is washed or specify its void space. Use the <a href="/drain-rock-calculator">drain rock calculator</a> to account for top cover and pipe displacement.
    </p>

    <h3>2. Residential Driveway Top Dressing</h3>
    <p>
      Angular crushed #57 stone can be used as a surface or drainage layer in some driveways. For a compacted driving base, a specified dense-graded material with fines is often a different choice. Rounded #57 gravel behaves differently under tire loads; match the aggregate to the planned layer and site.
    </p>

    <h3>3. Concrete and Asphalt Coarse Aggregate</h3>
    <p>
      A producer may use #57-size aggregate in a concrete or asphalt mixture when the full material meets that mix's specification. The size number alone does not establish concrete strength or asphalt performance.
    </p>

    <h2>Number 57 Stone vs Crusher Run: The Critical Distinction</h2>
    <p>
      Contractors frequently confuse #57 stone with crusher run (also called dense graded aggregate or quarry process). The difference is fundamental:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>#57 Stone:</strong> A size designation. Specify a clean, low-fines version where open drainage is needed; confirm whether it is angular or rounded.</li>
      <li><strong>Crusher Run:</strong> A dense-graded blend with fine particles that can compact into a base. Check the local product gradation rather than substituting it for specified drain stone.</li>
    </ul>
